# Bullet 1 — OOP Principles & MVC Architecture

> *"Designed a Java application using OOP principles and MVC architecture, separating validation, rendering, and persistence responsibilities for maintainable modularity."*

## 1.1 OOP Principles in Action

### Inheritance

| Base | Subclass | File |
|---|---|---|
| `Resume` (abstract) | `FresherResume` | [FresherResume.java](file:///c:/Users/shrut/Downloads/Resume%20Generator/src/com/resumegenerator/resume/FresherResume.java) |
| `Resume` (abstract) | `ExperiencedResume` | [ExperiencedResume.java](file:///c:/Users/shrut/Downloads/Resume%20Generator/src/com/resumegenerator/resume/ExperiencedResume.java) |

[Resume.java](file:///c:/Users/shrut/Downloads/Resume%20Generator/src/com/resumegenerator/resume/Resume.java) defines the abstract base with a `User` field, template composition, and the abstract method `getFormattedResume()`. Subclasses inherit the `user` field and `renderWithTemplate()` behavior, only overriding the formatting logic specific to their resume type.

### Polymorphism

The **Strategy pattern** via [ResumeTemplate.java](file:///c:/Users/shrut/Downloads/Resume%20Generator/src/com/resumegenerator/resume/ResumeTemplate.java) — a single interface with three interchangeable implementations:

```
ResumeTemplate template = TemplateFactory.create(TemplateType.MODERN);
template.render(user);  // dispatches to ModernTemplate.render() at runtime
```

All three concrete templates ([ClassicTemplate](file:///c:/Users/shrut/Downloads/Resume%20Generator/src/com/resumegenerator/resume/ClassicTemplate.java), [ModernTemplate](file:///c:/Users/shrut/Downloads/Resume%20Generator/src/com/resumegenerator/resume/ModernTemplate.java), [MinimalTemplate](file:///c:/Users/shrut/Downloads/Resume%20Generator/src/com/resumegenerator/resume/MinimalTemplate.java)) are substitutable through the same interface — the caller never knows which concrete class is executing.

### Encapsulation

[LoginUser.java](file:///c:/Users/shrut/Downloads/Resume%20Generator/src/com/resumegenerator/model/LoginUser.java) demonstrates textbook encapsulation:
- All 4 fields are `private` (`userId`, `username`, `email`, `password`)
- Access only through public getters/setters
- No-arg + parameterized constructors for flexible creation
- Future validation can be added inside setters without changing callers

### Abstraction

[Resume.java](file:///c:/Users/shrut/Downloads/Resume%20Generator/src/com/resumegenerator/resume/Resume.java) is an `abstract class` — it defines the contract (`getFormattedResume()`) and shared behavior (`renderWithTemplate()`) without specifying how a fresher vs. experienced resume is formatted. Consumers work with the `Resume` type, not the concrete subclass.

