### Foreign-Key Relationships

| Parent Table | Child Table | FK Column | ON DELETE |
|---|---|---|---|
| `users` | `resumes` | `user_id` | CASCADE |
| `resumes` | `education` | `resume_id` | CASCADE |
| `resumes` | `resume_skills` | `resume_id` | CASCADE |
| `skills` | `resume_skills` | `skill_id` | CASCADE |
| `resumes` | `experience` | `resume_id` | CASCADE |
| `resumes` | `projects` | `resume_id` | CASCADE |
| `resumes` | `certifications` | `resume_id` | CASCADE |
| `resumes` | `generated_resumes` | `resume_id` | CASCADE |

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Many-to-Many**: `skills ↔ resume_skills ↔ resumes` — one resume lists many skills; one skill appears on many resumes. The `resume_skills` junction table has a composite primary key `(resume_id, skill_id)`.

### Version Tracking — `generated_resumes` Table

Every PDF generation event is logged with `resume_id`, `file_path`, `file_format`, and `generated_at` timestamp. This provides a **complete audit trail** of every resume version ever produced.

## 2.3 Profile Management — SQL Query Logic

### UserDAO `save()` — Multi-Table Transactional INSERT

[UserDAO.java](file:///c:/Users/shrut/Downloads/Resume%20Generator/src/com/resumegenerator/dao/UserDAO.java) (~1165 lines) executes a **7-table cascade insert** using `PreparedStatement` + `RETURN_GENERATED_KEYS`:

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant RB as ResumeBuilder (UI)
    participant DAO as UserDAO
    participant DB as MySQL

    RB->>DAO: save(user)
    DAO->>DB: INSERT INTO users (full_name, email, phone) → user_id
    DAO->>DB: INSERT INTO resumes (user_id, title, type, objective) → resume_id
    DAO->>DB: INSERT INTO education (resume_id, institution, degree, start_year)
    loop Each Skill
        DAO->>DB: INSERT IGNORE INTO skills (skill_name)
        DAO->>DB: SELECT skill_id FROM skills WHERE skill_name = ?
        DAO->>DB: INSERT INTO resume_skills (resume_id, skill_id, display_order)
    end
    DAO->>DB: INSERT INTO experience (resume_id, company, title, start_date, description)
    DAO->>DB: INSERT INTO projects (resume_id, project_name)
    DAO->>DB: INSERT INTO certifications (resume_id, certification_name)
    DAO-->>RB: return user_id
```

**SQL Constants Defined in UserDAO:**

| Constant | SQL | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `INSERT_USER_SQL` | `INSERT INTO users (full_name, email, phone) VALUES (?, ?, ?)` | Profile creation |
| `INSERT_RESUME_SQL` | `INSERT INTO resumes (user_id, title, resume_type, objective) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)` | Resume metadata |
| `INSERT_EDUCATION_SQL` | `INSERT INTO education (resume_id, institution, degree, start_year) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)` | Education records |
| `INSERT_SKILL_SQL` | `INSERT IGNORE INTO skills (skill_name) VALUES (?)` | Deduplicated skill master |
| `SELECT_SKILL_ID_SQL` | `SELECT skill_id FROM skills WHERE skill_name = ?` | Skill ID lookup |
| `INSERT_RESUME_SKILL_SQL` | `INSERT INTO resume_skills (resume_id, skill_id, display_order) VALUES (?, ?, ?)` | Many-to-many junction |
| `INSERT_EXPERIENCE_SQL` | `INSERT INTO experience (...) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?)` | Work history |
| `INSERT_PROJECT_SQL` | `INSERT INTO projects (resume_id, project_name) VALUES (?, ?)` | Project records |
| `INSERT_CERTIFICATION_SQL` | `INSERT INTO certifications (resume_id, certification_name) VALUES (?, ?)` | Certification records |

### LoginDAO — Authentication CRUD

[LoginDAO.java](file:///c:/Users/shrut/Downloads/Resume%20Generator/src/com/resumegenerator/dao/LoginDAO.java):

| Method | SQL | Returns |
|---|---|---|
| `registerUser(LoginUser)` | `INSERT INTO login_users (username, email, password_hash) VALUES (?, ?, ?)` | `boolean` — success/failure |
| `authenticate(username)` | `SELECT id, username, email, password_hash FROM login_users WHERE username = ?` | `LoginUser` with stored hash (or `null`) |

> [!NOTE]
> **Why SELECT by username only (not username + password)?** The password is stored as a BCrypt hash. You can't put the plain-text password in a `WHERE` clause — MySQL would compare `"1234"` against `"$2a$10$N9qo8..."` and never match. Instead, the DAO returns the hash, and `AuthenticationService` calls `BCrypt.checkpw()`.

